import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris
from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA

# Step 1: Load Iris dataset
iris = load_iris()
X = iris.data
y = iris.target   # true labels (species)

# Step 2: Apply K-Means clustering (k=3)
kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3, random_state=42)
clusters = kmeans.fit_predict(X)

print("Cluster Centers:\n", kmeans.cluster_centers_)

# Step 3: Apply PCA to reduce to 2D
pca = PCA(n_components=2)
X_pca = pca.fit_transform(X)

# Step 4: Visualize clusters
plt.figure(figsize=(8,6))
plt.scatter(X_pca[:,0], X_pca[:,1], c=clusters, cmap='viridis', s=50)
plt.scatter(pca.transform(kmeans.cluster_centers_)[:,0],
            pca.transform(kmeans.cluster_centers_)[:,1],
            c='red', marker='X', s=200, label='Cluster Centers')
plt.xlabel("PCA Component 1")
plt.ylabel("PCA Component 2")
plt.title("K-Means Clusters on Iris Dataset (PCA 2D)")
plt.legend()
plt.show()

# Step 5: Compare predicted clusters vs true labels
comparison_df = pd.DataFrame({"True Label": y, "Predicted Cluster": clusters})
print(comparison_df.head(15))
🔑 Key Points
K-Means (k=3): Groups the Iris dataset into 3 clusters (matching the 3 species).

Cluster Centers: Mean feature values of each cluster in the original 4D space.

PCA: Reduces dimensions to 2D for visualization.

Comparison: You can check how well the predicted clusters align with the true species labels.

